      "Abstract": {"AbstractText": [
        {
          "NlmCategory": "OBJECTIVE",
          "Label": "OBJECTIVE",
          "content": "Whether or not breast-feeding is protective against asthma among children is still controversial. Therefore, we examined the effects of breast-feeding on hospitalization for asthma in early childhood."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "DESIGN",
          "content": "Secondary data analyses of a nationwide longitudinal survey of children in Japan ongoing since 2001, with results collected from 2001 to 2004. We used logistic regression models to evaluate the associations of breast-feeding with hospitalization for asthma in children between the ages of 6 and 42 months, adjusting for children's factors (sex, day-care attendance and presence of older siblings) and maternal factors (educational attainment and smoking habit). Setting All over Japan."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "SUBJECTS",
          "content": "Term singleton children with information on feeding practices during infancy (n 43367)."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "RESULTS",
          "Label": "RESULTS",
          "content": "After adjusting for maternal factors and children's factors, exclusive breast-feeding at 6-7 months of age was associated with decreased risk of hospitalization for asthma in children. The adjusted odds ratio was 0.77 (95% CI 0.56, 1.06). One-month longer duration of breast-feeding was associated with a 4% decreased risk of hospitalization for asthma (OR = 0.96; 95% CI 0.92, 0.99)."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "CONCLUSIONS",
          "Label": "CONCLUSIONS",
          "content": "The protective effects of breast-feeding on hospitalization for asthma were observed in children between the ages of 6 and 42 months."
        }
      ]},
